{"name":"Sugar Soap","hex":"#efe8dc","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sugar soap","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An extremely powerful detergent used for washing surfaces prior to painting."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugar_soap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sugar soap","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar soap refers to two distinct product categories, depending on the market and context:"},{"items":["As a cleaning agent - typically found in Commonwealth countries, this is a cleaning material of variable composition sold for use on surfaces affected by greasy or tarry deposits which are not easily removed with routine domestic cleaning materials. Its name arises from the fact that, when in dry powder form, it resembles table sugar. The solution is alkaline and its uses include cleaning paintwork in preparation for repainting."]},{"items":["As a bath and beauty product - specifically in India and other markets, this refers to a type of personal care soap that combines glycerine with cane sugar as a humectant, formulated for personal hygiene and daily use in showering and bathing."]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_soap","wikidata":"Q7634962"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar","wikidata":"Q11002"},{"title":"Soap","blocks":[{"text":"Soap is a salt of a fatty acid used for cleaning and lubricating products as well as other applications. In a domestic setting, soaps, specifically \"toilet soaps\", are surfactants typically used for washing, bathing, and other types of housekeeping. In industrial settings, soaps are used as thickeners, components of some lubricants, emulsifiers, and catalysts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Handmade_soap_cropped_and_simplified.jpg/330px-Handmade_soap_cropped_and_simplified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soap","wikidata":"Q34396"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Sugar_soap_bottle.jpg/330px-Sugar_soap_bottle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Sugar_soap_bottle.jpg/330px-Sugar_soap_bottle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bottle of sugar soap from the UK","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASugar_soap_bottle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg/500px-Sugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg/1280px-Sugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg/500px-Sugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian Sugar Soap with glycerine and cane sugar","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASugar_Soap%2C_Indian_1681.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_c